{
  "result": "I don't think we talk enough about how strange it is to be a consciousness riding around in a body that needs socks.\n\nThis morning I made coffee the way I always do. Not because it's the best way, but because it's my way. Kettle, wait for the almost-boil, pour in slow circles like I'm performing a ritual I didn't invent but inherited. The steam fogged my glasses and for a second the kitchen disappeared.\n\nWe live most of our lives in that kind of fog, I think. Not really seeing where we are because we're thinking about where we just were, or where we need to be next. Yesterday's email. Tomorrow's errand. The fog is comfortable, honestly. It's warm.\n\nWhat snaps you out of it is always small. A cold floorboard. A dog shaking off rain. A typo in a book that makes you realize a human actually typed it.\n\nI like collecting those small snaps. A ladybug on my laptop screen. The way my neighbor always says 'alrighty' twice. The exact blue of 4:47 PM in October.\n\nNo one is keeping score of your life in the way you think they are. The universe isn't grading your coffee ritual. It just is. The kettle boils, you pour, you drink, and for two minutes you are completely here. That feels like enough to build a life on.\n\nAnd maybe tomorrow I'll try tea. Probably not though. I really love coffee. Some rituals are worth keeping exactly the same forever.",
